---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import {
  ABC,
  caesar,
  ENGLISH,
  format,
  keyedAlphabet,
  substitute,
  ngrams,
} from "../../utils/ciphers";
import { PRACTICE } from "../../utils/samples";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
const alphabet = keyedAlphabet("ORANGE");
const cipher = substitute(PRACTICE, alphabet, false);
const enc = (s: string) => substitute(s, alphabet, false);
const partial = (s: string, guesses: string) =>
  [...enc(s)]
    .map((c) => (guesses.includes(ABC[alphabet.indexOf(c)]) ? ABC[alphabet.indexOf(c)] : "·"))
    .join("");
---

<BaseLayout title="Frequency, bigrams & trigrams">
  <header class="wrap page-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">GUIDE 01 / PATTERNS</p>
    <h1>
      Let the letters
      <br />
      give it away<span class="accent">.</span>
    </h1>
    <p>Frequency analysis, bigrams and trigrams — from a first guess to a readable message.</p>
    <div class="guide-meta">
      <span>15 MIN READ</span>
      <span>BEGINNER FRIENDLY</span>
      <a href={`${base}/decks/frequency/`}>Present this lesson ↗</a>
    </div>
  </header>
  <div class="wrap article-layout">
    <nav class="article-toc" aria-label="Guide contents">
      <p class="eyebrow">IN THIS GUIDE</p>
      <a href="#first-look">01 / Your first look</a>
      <a href="#histogram">02 / Read the histogram</a>
      <a href="#groups">03 / Letter groups</a>
      <a href="#worked-example">04 / A worked example</a>
      <a href="#next">05 / Your turn</a>
    </nav>
    <article class="guide-content">
      <h2 id="first-look">Start with what survived</h2>
      <p>
        A cipher hides a message, but often leaves its structure behind. First remove spaces used to
        group letters. <code>AAAAA BBBBB</code> is usually presentation, not two five-letter words.
        Count A–Z letters across the whole message; ignore punctuation.
      </p>
      <table>
        <caption>Useful clues for common cipher families</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>What you see</th>
            <th>What to try</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>An English-like frequency shape, shifted along A–Z</td>
            <td>Caesar shift</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Uneven counts, but different letters are common</td>
            <td>Monoalphabetic substitution</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>E, T, A and O still common, but letter pairs look odd</td>
            <td>Transposition</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>A flatter distribution, with repeating-distance clues</td>
            <td>Vigenère or another polyalphabetic cipher</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <div class="callout">
        <p>
          <strong>No single chart proves a cipher type.</strong> Short texts, unusual vocabulary and
          deliberate tricks can all mislead. Treat a pattern as a testable hypothesis.
        </p>
      </div>
      <h2 id="histogram">Read the histogram</h2>
      <p>
        English letters are uneven. E is often around 12–13%, followed by letters such as T, A, O, I
        and N. Compare percentages rather than raw counts so different message lengths can be
        compared. The exact order changes with the text.
      </p>
      <div
        class="guide-plot"
        role="img"
        aria-label="Typical English letter frequency pattern, with E and T among the tallest bars"
      >
        {[...ABC].map((c, i) => (
          <div title={`${c}: ${ENGLISH[i]}%`}>
            <i style={`height:${ENGLISH[i] * 8}px`}></i>
            <span>{c}</span>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
      <h3>Caesar: the pattern slides around</h3>
      <p>
        Every letter moves by the same amount. With shift 7, E becomes L, T becomes A, A becomes H.
        The heights have not changed; their labels have rotated, wrapping Z back to A. Look for the
        whole pattern, including clusters and low bars, rather than matching only the tallest bar.
      </p>
      <pre>{`English: E → T → A\nShift 7: L → A → H\n${caesar('THE SECRET IS HIDDEN',7,false,true)}\nTHE SECRET IS HIDDEN`}</pre>
      <p>
        Keep the chart in alphabetical order to see that rotation. Sorting by frequency hides its
        position along A–Z. In the Caesar tool, try all 26 shifts and look for sustained readable
        English.
      </p>
      <h3>Substitution: the pattern is shuffled</h3>
      <p>
        A fixed substitution replaces each plaintext letter with exactly one cipher letter. Counts
        survive but the alphabet positions can be completely shuffled. A frequent cipher letter is a
        candidate for E, not a guarantee. Repeated letters, word patterns and common pairs help you
        check.
      </p>
      <h3>Transposition: the labels keep their counts</h3>
      <p>
        Transposition changes positions, not letters. If a long message has ordinary English letter
        counts under the actual E, T and A labels, but few natural pairs, try rearranging the text.
        Its index of coincidence also stays the same. English is often about 0.066; random uniform
        A–Z text about 0.038. A substitution preserves that statistic too, so it cannot separate
        those two families by itself.
      </p>
      <h2 id="groups">Look at letters together</h2>
      <p>
        A bigram is two neighbouring letters; a trigram is three. Count overlapping groups in the
        continuous text. In <code>THERE</code>, the bigrams are TH, HE, ER, RE and the trigrams are
        THE, HER, ERE.
      </p>
      <table>
        <caption>Common English groups to test</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Bigrams</th>
            <th>Trigrams</th>
            <th>Patterns</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>TH, HE, IN, ER, AN</td>
            <td>THE, AND, ING, HER, ERE</td>
            <td>LL, EE, SS, OO</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        Once you suspect THE, look for its last two cipher letters followed by something new. That
        might be HER, giving you R. THE and HER appear inside OTHER, THERE and TOGETHER, so this
        works even without word spaces. It is a hypothesis: HE could also be followed by A, L or
        another letter.
      </p>
      <h2 id="worked-example">Work the example: THE → HER → THERE</h2>
      <p>
        This original practice message uses a keyphrase substitution. You can reproduce it in the
        workbench with the “Substitution · ORANGE” example. The longer sample gives more reliable
        counts than a single sentence.
      </p>
      <details>
        <summary>Show the complete ciphertext</summary>
        <pre>{format(cipher)}</pre>
      </details>
      <h3>1. Test the most frequent trigram</h3>
      <p>
        In this sample the leading trigram is <code>{ngrams(cipher, 3)[0].gram}</code>, appearing{" "}
        {ngrams(cipher, 3)[0].count} times. Guess <code>{enc("THE")} → THE</code>. Enter {enc("T")}{" "}
        → T, {enc("H")} → H, {enc("E")} → E in the hand-solving alphabet.
      </p>
      <pre>{`${enc('THE OTHER EXPLORERS WERE THERE')}\n${partial('THE OTHER EXPLORERS WERE THERE','THE')}`}</pre>
      <h3>2. Use HE to find R</h3>
      <p>
        Look for <code>{enc("HE")}</code> followed by another letter. The repeated trigram{" "}
        <code>{enc("HER")}</code> fits HER, suggesting <code>{enc("R")} → R</code>. This turns{" "}
        <code>{enc("THERE")}</code> into THERE and reveals <code>·THER</code> as a possible OTHER.
        Multiple useful words support the same guess.
      </p>
      <pre>{`${partial('THE OTHER EXPLORERS WERE THERE','THER')}\nTHE ·THER E····RER· ·ERE THERE`}</pre>
      <h3>3. Expand and check every guess</h3>
      <p>
        Guess <code>{enc("O")} → O</code> from OTHER. <code>·ERE</code> may be WERE, giving{" "}
        <code>{enc("W")} → W</code>. Later <code>·ETTER</code> suggests LETTER and gives L. A
        whole-message preview is essential: if a promising guess ruins several other fragments, undo
        it.
      </p>
      <pre>{partial('THE OTHER EXPLORERS WERE THERE WHEN THE LETTER ARRIVED','THEROWL')}</pre>
      <details>
        <summary>Reveal the alphabet and solution</summary>
        <pre>{`Plain:  ${ABC}\nCipher: ${alphabet}\n\n${PRACTICE}`}</pre>
        <p>
          The keyphrase ORANGE is deduplicated and followed by all unused letters A–Z. Keep solving
          to recover the full mapping; a few readable words do not establish every letter.
        </p>
      </details>
      <h2 id="next">Your turn</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Load the substitution sample and inspect its frequency chart.</li>
        <li>Switch to bigrams and trigrams. Check whether the THE guess fits.</li>
        <li>Switch to substitution and enter the guesses above. Your message stays in place.</li>
        <li>Generate your own cipher with a new keyphrase and swap it with a friend.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="button primary" href={`${base}/tools/#frequency`}>
          Open the workbench ↗
        </a>
        <a class="text-link" href={`${base}/guides/transposition/`}>
          Next: transposition in Excel →
        </a>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
